---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import BitStrip from '../components/BitStrip.astro';
import ChainsTable from '../components/ChainsTable.astro';
import ProductsTable from '../components/ProductsTable.astro';
import Examples from '../components/Examples.astro';
import GroupTable from '../components/GroupTable.astro';
import Puzzle from '../components/Puzzle.astro';
import RuleWall from '../components/RuleWall.astro';
import FailureTable from '../components/FailureTable.astro';
import rounds from '../../public/data/rounds.json';
import { progress } from '../lib/data';
import { pct, thousands } from '../lib/format';

type Trace = {
  relation: string;
  context: number[][];
  examples: { a: number; b: number; target: number; prediction: number; rounds: { p: number[] }[] }[];
};
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const product = (rounds as Trace[]).find((t) => t.relation === 'a*b')!;
const rows = product.context.slice(0, 4) as [number, number, number][];
const question = product.examples[0]!;
const guesses = question.rounds.map((r) => r.p);
const wide = progress.wide;
const w32 = wide.widths['32']!;
const conditionalRow = progress.groups?.find((g) => g.key === 'T4')?.relations.find((r) => r.name === 'a>b?a-b:a+b');
const conditional = conditionalRow?.exact ?? w32.families['a>b?a-b:a+b'];
const size = progress.current?.parameters ?? wide.parameters;
const release = progress.release?.results ?? {};
---

<Layout
  title="Teenie: in-context learning of hidden integer functions"
  description="A 1.1M-parameter research model that infers hidden integer functions from 999 examples and predicts exact 16- and 32-bit answers, with honest confidence."
  keywords={['few-shot learning', 'function inference from examples', 'bit-level transformer', 'learning multiplication', '32-bit integers']}
  kind="website"
>
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Teenie · a research experiment by <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/sri-pranav-tene">Sri Pranav Tene</a></p>
    <h1>Show it 999 examples. It works out the rule.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Teenie is a small neural network, 1.1 million numbers in all, trained on a laptop. Give it examples of a hidden
      rule and it answers questions it has never seen, without anyone telling it the rule. Have a go at one of its
      puzzles first, then see what it said.
    </p>
    <p class="cta">
      <a class="button" href={`${base}/try/`}>Try it with your own rule</a>
      <a class="button quiet" href="#where">How good is it?</a>
      <a class="button quiet" href="#limits">How bad is it?</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <Puzzle />

  <h2>Rules it cracks</h2>
  <p>
    Chains of steps most people couldn't spot from a list of numbers. Teenie works each one out from examples, and here
    is how often it got a fresh question exactly right. Where it slips, the card says so. Click one to test it yourself.
  </p>
  <RuleWall />

  <section class="stats grid-3">
    <div class="card stat">
      <span class="label">Model size</span>
      <span class="value">{size ? `${(size / 1e6).toFixed(2)}M` : '—'}</span>
      <span class="note">{size ? `${thousands(size)} parameters in the current model, the same at every width up to 32 bits` : ''}</span>
    </div>
    <div class="card stat">
      <span class="label">An unseen conditional at 32 bits</span>
      <span class="value">{pct(conditional)}</span>
      <span class="note">{`exactly right on a>b ? a−b : a+b, which it never trained on, with ${conditionalRow?.recovered?.toFixed(1) ?? '—'} of 32 bits recovered against lookup's ${conditionalRow?.lookup?.toFixed(1) ?? '—'}`}</span>
    </div>
    <div class="card stat">
      <span class="label">Multiplying 32-bit numbers it infers</span>
      <span class="value">{pct(release['a*b']?.exact)}</span>
      <span class="note">{`exactly right on a*b, and ${pct(release['(a*b)+a']?.exact)} on (a*b)+a, which it never trained on. It can't yet multiply values it computes first, like (a+b)*a.`}</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <h2 id="where">Where it stands</h2>
  <p>
    The current model on every test group at 32 bits. It never trained on any of these rules. <b>Bits recovered</b>
    measures how far its guess beats chance, from 0 for a blind guess to 32 for certain and right, and <b>lookup</b>
    gets the same score for the best of five methods that understand nothing (picked after the fact, which flatters
    them). <b>Sure → right</b> says how often it was right when it was sure of every bit. Click a group to see the
    rules in it.
  </p>
  <GroupTable />
  <p class="small muted">
    32 episodes × 16 questions per relation; <code>uv run teenie-bits-eval --tests mix7 --width 32</code>. The
    middle band (rules with no textbook solution) is compared against models fitted per task on the
    <a href="/results/">Results</a> page.
  </p>

  <h2>Multi-step chains</h2>
  <p>
    Chains are a start such as <code>a+b</code> or <code>a*b</code> followed by one or two steps, each one of 21 kinds
    (<code>+ − ^ | &amp; *</code> with <code>a</code>, <code>b</code> or a random constant, shifts, not), with a fresh
    random constant each time. The current model trained on chains too, but every start and first step in this table
    is a pairing it never saw. <b>Beyond lookup</b> counts only the questions that every lookup method gets wrong, and
    <b>sure</b> means its least certain bit is at 0.99 or above.
  </p>
  <ChainsTable />
  <p class="small muted">48 episodes × 16 questions per kind; <code>uv run python -m teenie.bits.chain_split</code>.</p>

  <h2>Learning to compute through a product</h2>
  <p>
    Chains through a product at 16 bits, on held-out start and step pairs, as training went on. A model that can't
    compute the product falls back on matching examples by the input bits that matter, and since the high bits of a
    product depend on almost all of them, 999 examples stop matching anything around bit 5. Scratch registers (numbers
    it writes and reads back every round) were the first change that got it past that.
  </p>
  <ProductsTable />
  <p class="small muted">
    {`After 32-bit training with 12 rounds, the current model gets a*b ${pct(release['a*b']?.exact)} and (a*b)+a ${pct(release['(a*b)+a']?.exact)} exactly right at 32 bits. Products of values it has to compute first, like (a+b)*a or a*a − b*b, are still right only on their lowest ~6 bits.`}
  </p>

  <h2 id="limits">How bad is it?</h2>
  <p>
    Bad enough to be worth showing. These rules look simple, and Teenie gets most of them wrong almost every time.
    Each percentage is how often it got a fresh question exactly right, out of 256. Click a rule to watch it fail.
  </p>
  <FailureTable />

  <h3>In short</h3>
  <div class="grid-3">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Works: combining steps it knows</h3>
      <p class="small">
        Chains of additions, subtractions, bitwise operations, shifts and constants, plus conditionals it never trained
        on. At 32 bits most of these are questions lookup can't answer, and when it's sure, it's right.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Partly: products</h3>
      <p class="small">
        It multiplies its inputs and uses the product in later steps (<code>a*b</code>, <code>(a*b)+a</code>, chains
        that start from a product, at 32 bits), but cannot yet multiply values it computes first:
        <code>(a+b)*a</code> or <code>a*a − b*b</code> get only their lowest few bits. A day of training on exactly
        those products barely moved them, so the fix has to come from the model's structure rather than more data.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Knows when it's guessing</h3>
      <p class="small">
        Its confidence is the certainty of its least certain bit. On rules it can't work out it's rarely sure, so the
        answers it is sure of stay right even where its overall score is low.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="examples">What it trains on</h2>
  <p>
    The kinds of rule it practised, written out, and the held-out middle band it's tested on. Open a group to see a
    few of its rules.
  </p>
  <Examples />

  <h2>How it answers</h2>
  <section class="demo card" id="demo" data-guesses={JSON.stringify(guesses)} aria-label="An 8-bit model answering a multiplication question, round by round">
    <div class="demo-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">999 examples (4 shown) · relation hidden</p>
        <div class="rows">
          {
            rows.map(([a, b, c]) => (
              <div class="ex">
                <BitStrip value={a} />
                <span class="op">?</span>
                <BitStrip value={b} />
                <span class="op">→</span>
                <BitStrip value={c} />
              </div>
            ))
          }
          <div class="ex dots">⋮ 995 more</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">New question · <span data-role="round">round 1 of {guesses.length}</span></p>
        <div class="ex">
          <BitStrip value={question.a} />
          <span class="op">?</span>
          <BitStrip value={question.b} />
          <span class="op">→</span>
          <div class="answer">
            <BitStrip value={0} probs={guesses[0]} />
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="small muted note">
          A recording from the earlier 8-bit model, small enough to show: it guesses every bit at once and corrects the
          guess each round by reading the examples again. Here the hidden relation is <code>a*b</code>:
          {` ${question.a} × ${question.b} = ${question.target} (mod 256).`} Blue = surely 1, pale = surely 0. 8-bit results
          are not used to judge the model: with only 256 possible answers and examples covering 1.5% of all inputs,
          lookup alone does well there.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <h2 id="next">What's next: Mega</h2>
  <div class="card mega">
    <p>
      Teenie answers from 999 examples with 1.1M parameters trained on a laptop GPU. <b>Mega</b> is the scaled-up
      version, and it goes after the problems this page lists rather than just adding size:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>multiplying values it computes itself, like <code>(a+b)*a</code>, which more training of Teenie couldn't fix;</li>
      <li>rotations and real bit mixing, where Teenie is at chance;</li>
      <li>longer chains of steps and rules with more inputs than <code>a</code> and <code>b</code>;</li>
      <li>a fair comparison with TabPFN, and then examples with noise in them.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="small muted">
      Progress will show up on this site as it happens, the runs that fail included. The <a href="/changelog/">changelog</a>
      and the <a href="/findings/">findings</a> get every result first.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Read more</h2>
  <div class="grid-2 links">
    <a class="card link" href={`${base}/model/`}>
      <p class="eyebrow">Model</p>
      <h3>The bit-token design</h3>
      <p>One token per answer bit, refined over rounds; the architecture and where its parameters are.</p>
    </a>
    <a class="card link" href={`${base}/results/`}>
      <p class="eyebrow">Results</p>
      <h3>How it compares</h3>
      <p>Rules with no textbook solution, scored against lookups and against models fitted to each task.</p>
    </a>
    <a class="card link" href={`${base}/writeup/`}>
      <p class="eyebrow">Write-up</p>
      <h3>The short version</h3>
      <p>What it does, how it's scored, where it stops, and what we'd try next.</p>
    </a>
    <a class="card link" href={`${base}/changelog/`}>
      <p class="eyebrow">Changelog</p>
      <h3>How it got here</h3>
      <p>Every experiment in order, failures included.</p>
    </a>
  </div>
</Layout>

<script>
  // Replay the recorded guesses: all 8 bits update together, round after round.
  const demo = document.getElementById('demo');
  const reduce = matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;
  if (demo) {
    const guesses = JSON.parse(demo.dataset.guesses ?? '[]') as number[][];
    const cells = Array.from(demo.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.answer .cell'));
    const label = demo.querySelector('[data-role="round"]')!;
    const show = (t: number) => {
      const p = guesses[t]!;
      cells.forEach((c, i) => c.style.setProperty('--p', String(p[7 - i])));
      label.textContent = `round ${t + 1} of ${guesses.length}`;
    };
    if (reduce) show(guesses.length - 1);
    else {
      let step = 0;
      show(0);
      window.setInterval(() => {
        step = (step + 1) % (guesses.length + 3); // hold the final answer for a moment
        show(Math.min(step, guesses.length - 1));
      }, 700);
    }
  }
</script>

<style>
  .cta {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }

  .cta .button {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 0.6rem 1.1rem;
    border-radius: 8px;
    background: var(--series-1);
    color: #fff;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .cta .button.quiet {
    background: none;
    color: var(--ink);
    border: 1px solid var(--axis);
  }

  .mega ul {
    margin: 0.4rem 0 0.6rem;
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.25rem;
  }

  .hero {
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .demo-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 400px), 1fr));
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }

  .rows {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.35rem;
  }

  .ex {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    flex-wrap: nowrap;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .ex :global(.strip .dec) {
    display: none;
  }

  .ex :global(.strip .cells) {
    gap: 2px;
  }

  .ex :global(.strip .cell) {
    width: 13px;
    height: 20px;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    border-radius: 3px;
  }

  .answer :global(.strip .cell) {
    transition: background 0.35s;
  }

  .op {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-family: var(--mono);
  }

  .dots {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    padding-left: 0.25rem;
  }

  .note {
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }

  .scroll {
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .stats {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .link {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: border-color 0.15s;
  }

  .link:hover {
    border-color: var(--series-1);
  }

  .link h3 {
    margin-top: 0;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .link p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
</style>
